---
import ToolPage from '../../components/tools/ToolPage.astro';
import { baristaNumbers } from '../../tools/engine/barista';
import { money, pct } from '../../tools/format';

const m = (x: number) => money(x, 'USD');
// The article's worked example, computed by the calculator's own engine code at build time.
const base = { age: 45, spend: 40_000, ptUntil: 60, planTo: 90, ret: 3, tax: 0, timing: 'start', cur: 'USD' } as const;
const ex = await baristaNumbers({ ...base, pt: 15_000 });
const table = await Promise.all(
  [0, 10_000, 15_000, 20_000, 30_000].map(async (pt) => {
    const r = await baristaNumbers({ ...base, pt });
    return [m(pt), m(r.formulaBarista), m(r.barista), pct(1 - r.barista / r.full, 1)];
  }),
);
const gap = ex.formulaBarista - ex.barista;

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is Barista FIRE?',
    a: '<p>Early retirement with a bridge: you leave full-time work but keep some paid work, so the portfolio only has to cover the gap between your spending and that pay. The name is a label for part-time work chosen for flexibility or benefits rather than for pay.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is the answer higher than (spending − part-time pay) × 25?',
    a: '<p>That shortcut assumes the part-time pay lasts for the whole retirement, because the 25× rule assumes a gap that lasts about 30 years. Most people do not plan to work part-time at 80. Once the pay has an end date, the later years need the full spending from the portfolio, and the saving is smaller.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I enter part-time pay before or after tax?',
    a: '<p>After tax. The calculator’s flat tax option applies only to withdrawals from the portfolio.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does part-time income remove sequence risk?',
    a: '<p>No. It reduces the withdrawals in the early years, which softens a bad start, but the income can stop and the full spending still has to be funded afterwards. This calculator uses one steady return; the Monte Carlo calculator tests a plan against many sequences.</p>',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  id="barista-fire"
  heading="Barista FIRE calculator"
  lede="The portfolio you need when you leave full-time work, if part-time pay covers some of your spending for a number of years, compared with full retirement. It runs the Glideworth planning engine in your browser; nothing you type is sent anywhere."
  faq={faq}
  adds="This calculator has one income that stops at one age and a steady return."
  related={[
    { href: '/blog/what-is-your-fire-number/', label: 'What is your FIRE number? The 25x rule and its limits' },
    { href: '/features/branches-and-change-sets/', label: 'Branches: keep a part-time version of your plan next to the full one' },
    { href: '/features/plan-modelling/', label: 'What the planner can model' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>How the calculator works</h2>
  <p>
    The calculator describes your plan to the engine: spending every year from the age you leave full-time work to the
    age you plan to, part-time pay (after tax) until the age it stops, and your return after inflation. It then asks the
    engine’s solver for the <strong>smallest starting portfolio that pays every year in full</strong>. The solver scales
    the starting balance up and down, running the projection each time, until it finds the point where the last year is
    just paid. It does the same for full retirement (no part-time pay) so you can see what the pay is worth.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything is in today’s money. If the part-time pay is more than your spending, the surplus is saved into the
    portfolio.
  </p>

  <h2>The formula</h2>
  <p>
    The portfolio that exactly funds a plan is the present value of its yearly withdrawals, each discounted at the
    return after inflation:
  </p>
  <p class="formula">portfolio = Σ w<sub>t</sub> ÷ (1 + r)<sup>t + 1 − k</sup></p>
  <p>
    where <code>w<sub>t</sub></code> is year <code>t</code>’s spending minus part-time pay (grossed up for the flat tax,
    if any), counting from <code>t</code> = 0, and <code>k</code> is the share of the year a withdrawal is invested for:
    1 when withdrawals are at the start of each year, ½ in the middle, 0 at the end. With start-of-year withdrawals this
    is the familiar Σ w<sub>t</sub> ÷ (1 + r)<sup>t</sup>.
  </p>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    Computed by the calculator’s own code when this page was built, with the inputs of the
    <a href="/blog/barista-fire-part-time-income/">Barista FIRE article</a>: spending of {m(40_000)} from 45 to 90 (45
    years), part-time pay of {m(15_000)} after tax until 60, a steady 3% after inflation and withdrawals at the start of
    each year.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Full retirement at 45: formula {m(ex.formulaFull)}, engine {m(ex.full)}.</li>
    <li>
      Barista, pay stopping at 60: formula {m(ex.formulaBarista)}, engine {m(ex.barista)}, {pct(1 - ex.barista / ex.full, 1)}{' '}
      less than full retirement.
    </li>
    <li>The 25× shortcut: ({m(40_000)} − {m(15_000)}) × 25 = {m(ex.shortcut)}, which assumes the pay never stops.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-label="Table, scrolls sideways if it does not fit">
  <table>
    <caption>Part-time pay for 15 years, same plan (engine, with the formula for comparison)</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Part-time pay</th><th>Formula</th><th>Engine</th><th>Saving against full retirement</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>{table.map((r) => <tr>{r.map((c) => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Why the engine and the formula agree</h2>
  <p>
    For every timing option the engine and the formula agree to within the solver’s precision (a difference of {m(Math.abs(gap))} in the
    example above). Each withdrawal can only take what the portfolio holds at the moment the timing option says the
    money leaves, which is exactly what the formula assumes, including in the final year.
  </p>

  <h2>What this assumes and leaves out</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A steady return.</strong> The answer is a present value, not a safe-withdrawal calculation: it has no margin
      for bad markets. Use the <a href="/tools/monte-carlo-retirement-calculator/">Monte Carlo calculator</a> to test a
      portfolio against many sequences.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pay that stops on schedule.</strong> Part-time work can end early through redundancy, illness or a
      recession, which tend to arrive together with falling markets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What the full-time job provided.</strong> Health cover, employer pension contributions and a state pension
      record are not modelled; neither is any state pension later in life, which would lower the number.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tax.</strong> None unless you set the flat rate on withdrawals; part-time pay is entered after its own tax.
    </li>
  </ul>
</ToolPage>
